WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:06.900
Take a look at these meetings of world leaders
from the past ten years and you’ll likely

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.100
notice one figure.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.480
This is German Chancellor Angela Merkel, one
of the most influential leaders of Europe.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.810
Her leadership spans 13 years and four terms.

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:30.099
In that span, she became one of the most powerful
women in the world.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.640
But she recently stepped down as leader of
her party and she won’t be running for re-election.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.740
By 2021, not only will Germany lose one of
its longest-serving leaders — so will Europe.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.880
Merkel has dominated European politics for decades.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:54.700
So how did Angela Merkel become so powerful
and what does her exit mean for Europe?

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:07.190
The Berlin Wall divided Germany’s capital
in half for almost 3 decades.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:10.540
This side was part of the prosperous and democratic
West Germany.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:16.540
And the other side was the impoverished communist
East Germany - a satellite state of the Soviet Union.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.160
But when the Wall fell in 1989, the East unified
with the West.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.860
And Germany quickly became one of the largest
and most populous countries in Europe.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.280
This is when Angela Merkel entered politics.

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:34.859
She was elected to be a member of the new
parliament in 1990, but rapidly rose through

00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:35.859
the ranks.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.480
By ‘94 she was Minister of the Environment.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.180
And by 2000 she was head of her political
party, the CDU.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:49.020
While Merkel became more important in Germany,
reunited Germany became more important in Europe.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.880
It became a leader in the European Union.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:57.100
There were open borders between EU member
countries, to let people and trade pass through freely.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:03.080
And Germany led the effort to create the “Eurozone”
where 11 EU countries adopted the Euro as a

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.359
common currency in 1999.

00:02:05.359 --> 00:02:08.560
These policies made EU countries more dependent
on each other.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.340
And in the early 2000s, the EU increasingly
looked to Germany,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.260
one of its most powerful economies,

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.360
to be its leader.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.780
Starting in 2005, that leadership came
from Angela Merkel.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:26.260
Merkel was elected Germany’s Chancellor
and demonstrated a talent for building consensus.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.370
She engineered a “grand coalition” between
Germany’s biggest political parties

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.319
from the right and the left.

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:37.000
With this alliance behind her, Merkel solved
two of Germany’s biggest economic problems.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.200
She cut huge government spending and reduced
unemployment.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:44.599
Her ability to stabilize Germany's economy
became particularly important when the EU

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:45.600
faced a crisis.:

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.580
"This Eurozone crisis has gone from bad
to worse."

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.680
“It is a high stakes game where the players
are world leaders

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.800
and the wager is the world’s economy."

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.060
The global economic recession in 2008 hit
Greece especially hard.

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:02.120
The country inched towards bankruptcy and
its economic decline brought down the value

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.269
of the Euro, which hurt everyone in the Eurozone.

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:08.880
But Merkel’s Germany was weathering the
recession better than anyone in Europe.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.760
So it fell on Merkel to rescue Greece with
her economic strategy:

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.920
She demanded Greece pass huge spending cuts

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.680
in exchange for loans from the EU and the International Monetary Fund.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.880
These were deeply unpopular across Europe.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.260
Cutting Greece’s pensions and services were
harsh tactics.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.670
But Merkel’s priority was to retain the
integrity of the Eurozone and Greece’s debt

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.080
threatened that union:

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.359
Merkel eventually got the 16 Eurozone countries
to support her plan and kept the Eurozone

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.460
together, cementing her role as the unparalleled
but controversial leader of Europe.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.600
Within Germany, Merkel’s popularity continued
to grow.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:53.780
Germans called her "Mutti," meaning
"mother," for taking care of their economy.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:58.620
She even earned her own emoji, -&lt;&gt;- representing
her trademark hand-gesture.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.940
And in 2013, she won her third term as Chancellor,

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:06.120
but this term would turn out to be very different than the others.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.900
"The number of migrants coming in to Europe

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.980
this year alone is now at more than 500,000."

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:18.120
"Almost 900 people drowned as they attempted the crossing from Libya to the EU.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:24.300
"Some countries are greeting them with open arms. Others are setting up fences with barbed wire on top."

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:30.080
In 2015, more than 1 million refugees fled
conflicts in Northern Africa and the Middle East

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.460
and migrated to Europe.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:35.140
Merkel called on EU leaders to help take in
refugees.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.919
But several European nations pushed back,
as

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:42.210
nationalist politicians in these countries
stoked anti-immigrant fears.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.560
This time, Merkel could not build a consensus.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.640
Migrants piled up in Europe’s southern countries,
causing a humanitarian crisis while undermining

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.520
the solidarity of the EU.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.580
So Merkel acted alone.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:17.800
In 2015, Germany granted asylum to over 140
thousand migrants — more than any other

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.639
European country.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:21.120
At first things went well.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.120
33% of Germans said the country could take
on additional asylum-seekers.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:31.840
Many even met incoming trains carrying migrants
with support and supplies.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:35.539
But on New Year’s eve in 2015 the situation
changed.

00:05:35.539 --> 00:05:41.180
“A day after the allegations of mass sexual
assault were made public, Cologne continues

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:44.240
to search for the perpetrators and for answers.”

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:49.280
“The attackers are described as young men of Arab or
North African appearance.”

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.290
A rash of sexual assaults and thefts changed
public opinion.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:58.860
After the attacks in Cologne, only 18% of
Germans felt the country could take in more asylees.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.120
And German public opinion reversed on Merkel too.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.900
A far-right party called the Alternative for
Germany or AfD, seized on the anti-immigrant

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.949
sentiment among voters.

00:06:10.949 --> 00:06:17.819
1.5 million voters who had previously backed Merkel’s
Grand Coalition in 2013, switched their support

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.480
and voted for the AfD in 2017.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:26.380
Having lost support for her coalition government
in 2018, Merkel decided to step down as leader

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:31.740
of her party, though she’ll remain Germany’s
Chancellor until her term ends in 2021.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:37.080
Merkel’s party recently elected Annegret
Kramp-Karrenbauer, as its next leader.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:41.580
She’s a moderate Merkel ally who could
very well become Germany’s next Chancellor.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.660
But for the rest of Europe, Merkel's absence
could be more unsettling.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:51.600
Since the Eurozone and migrant crises, Europe
has seen nationalist political parties gain

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:56.130
popularity in recent elections, threatening
the unity of the EU.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:01.420
And Merkel has gone from being a champion
of a united Europe to its last remaining strong defender.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.940
With Merkel stepping away, these nationalist
leaders could gain influence over the future

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.420
of the European Union.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:13.560
And that’s something Merkel is very aware
of.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:31.020
So in this video, we touched briefly on the idea of a European identity.

